 workplace.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "legwork", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the hard tedious work that has to be done in order to achieve something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  Have you started doing any legwork for the assignment? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob02aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are words you use to talk about the research you do.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "get hold of ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to obtain", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: How else are we supposed to get hold of some information then?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob02aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "the scoop ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "information; news", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I've been frantically trying to get the scoop online!", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob02aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "accessible", "type": "name"}, {"content": "easy to obtain", "type": "definition"}, {"content": " (adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There isn't much accessible information online. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob02aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to read through ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to read all of something ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I'm reading through all the articles.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob02aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob02re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " The Perfect Infinitive: -ing Forms", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use perfect infinitives in two ways:", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "1. After link verbs like <span class=\"italic\">seem</span> and <span class=\"italic\">appear </span>to refer to a previous time period.<br/> 2. After phrases expressing emotions and feelings.<br/><br/>  When you talk about results and time, we can use an <span class=\"italic\">-ing</span> form to emphasize that one thing happens before another.<br/><br/>  For example:<br/><br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I didn't remember <span class=\"strong\">having met</span> her before.<br/>  <span class=\"strong\">Having</span> finally <span class=\"strong\">understood</span> what they meant, I began to do my work.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "After having spent hours last night frantically trying to get the scoop online, I went to bed. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I just would have preferred to have slept all night rather than reading through all those articles.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " Future Continuous: Stative Verbs", "type": "heading"}, {"content": " Normally, you do not use stative verbs in the continuous tense because they describe states rather than actions. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use the Future Continuous to talk about an unfinished action or event that will be in progress at a time later than now. <br/><br/>  There are some stative verbs you can use in the Future Continuous tense.<br/><br/>  1. When they have an active meaning<br/> <span class=\"italic\">I'll be tasting it to see if it is good or not.</span><br/><br/>  2. When they emphasize change or development<br/> <span class=\"italic\">More schools will be including exercise classes as part of their curriculum.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I'll be having a meeting with the author in a few days. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I'll be expecting a reply back today via email or phone telling me which day works for him.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Will I be hearing about this all day?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I'll be feeling much better too after I've had a good night's sleep.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I'll be thinking about what it could possibly be until he gets back.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Write down a list of verbs that change meaning in the Continuous tense. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a dialogue (12-15 lines) using 4 of these verbs. Use them in the Simple Future tense and the Future Continuous tense.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Proofread your dialogue.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Practice reading it aloud with a partner. ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I identify verbs that refer to a state.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I use verbs which change meaning when used to refer to actions that are unfinished.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I practice reading a dialogue aloud with my partner.", "type": "text"}]}]}